NNAAMMEE
       flock - manage locks from shell scripts

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       fflloocckk [options] _f_i_l_e|_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_s]

       fflloocckk [options] _f_i_l_e|_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y --cc _c_o_m_m_a_n_d

       fflloocckk [options] _n_u_m_b_e_r

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This utility manages fflloocckk(2) locks from within shell scripts or from
       the command line.

       The first and second of the above forms wrap the lock around the
       execution of a _c_o_m_m_a_n_d, in a manner similar to ssuu(1) or nneewwggrrpp(1). They
       lock a specified _f_i_l_e or _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y, which is created (assuming
       appropriate permissions) if it does not already exist. By default, if
       the lock cannot be immediately acquired, fflloocckk waits until the lock is
       available.

       The third form uses an open file by its file descriptor _n_u_m_b_e_r. See the
       examples below for how that can be used.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --cc, ----ccoommmmaanndd _c_o_m_m_a_n_d
           Pass a single _c_o_m_m_a_n_d, without arguments, to the shell with --cc.

       --EE, ----ccoonnfflliicctt--eexxiitt--ccooddee _n_u_m_b_e_r
           The exit status used when the --nn option is in use, and the
           conflicting lock exists, or the --ww option is in use, and the
           timeout is reached. The default value is 11. The _n_u_m_b_e_r has to be in
           the range of 0 to 255.

       --FF, ----nnoo--ffoorrkk
           Do not fork before executing _c_o_m_m_a_n_d. Upon execution the flock
           process is replaced by _c_o_m_m_a_n_d which continues to hold the lock.
           This option is incompatible with ----cclloossee as there would otherwise
           be nothing left to hold the lock.

       --ee, --xx, ----eexxcclluussiivvee
           Obtain an exclusive lock, sometimes called a write lock. This is
           the default.

       --nn, ----nnbb, ----nnoonnbblloocckk
           Fail rather than wait if the lock cannot be immediately acquired.
           See the --EE option for the exit status used.

       --oo, ----cclloossee
           Close the file descriptor on which the lock is held before
           executing _c_o_m_m_a_n_d. This is useful if _c_o_m_m_a_n_d spawns a child process
           which should not be holding the lock.

       --ss, ----sshhaarreedd
           Obtain a shared lock, sometimes called a read lock.

       --uu, ----uunnlloocckk
           Drop a lock. This is usually not required, since a lock is
           automatically dropped when the file is closed. However, it may be
           required in special cases, for example if the enclosed command
           group may have forked a background process which should not be
           holding the lock.

       --ww, ----wwaaiitt, ----ttiimmeeoouutt _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
           Fail if the lock cannot be acquired within _s_e_c_o_n_d_s. Decimal
           fractional values are allowed. See the --EE option for the exit
           status used. The zero number of _s_e_c_o_n_d_s is interpreted as
           ----nnoonnbblloocckk.

       ----vveerrbboossee
           Report how long it took to acquire the lock, or why the lock could
           not be obtained.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       The command uses <sysexits.h> exit status values for everything, except
       when using either of the options --nn or --ww which report a failure to
       acquire the lock with an exit status given by the --EE option, or 1 by
       default. The exit status given by --EE has to be in the range of 0 to
       255.

       When using the _c_o_m_m_a_n_d variant, and executing the child worked, then
       the exit status is that of the child command.

NNOOTTEESS
       fflloocckk does not detect deadlock. See fflloocckk(2) for details.

       Some file systems (e. g. NFS and CIFS) have a limited implementation of
       fflloocckk(2) and flock may always fail. For details see fflloocckk(2), nnffss(5)
       and mmoouunntt..cciiffss(8). Depending on mount options, flock can always fail
       there.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       Note that "shell> " in examples is a command line prompt.

       shell1> flock /tmp -c cat; shell2> flock -w .007 /tmp -c echo;
       /bin/echo $?
           Set exclusive lock to directory _/_t_m_p and the second command will
           fail.

       shell1> flock -s /tmp -c cat; shell2> flock -s -w .007 /tmp -c echo;
       /bin/echo $?
           Set shared lock to directory _/_t_m_p and the second command will not
           fail. Notice that attempting to get exclusive lock with second
           command would fail.

       shell> flock -x local-lock-file echo 'a b c'
           Grab the exclusive lock "local-lock-file" before running echo with
           'a b c'.

       (; flock -n 9 || exit 1; # ... commands executed under lock ...; )
       9>/var/lock/mylockfile
           The form is convenient inside shell scripts. The mode used to open
           the file doesn’t matter to fflloocckk; using _> or _>_> allows the lockfile
           to be created if it does not already exist, however, write
           permission is required. Using _< requires that the file already
           exists but only read permission is required.

       [ "${FLOCKER}" != "$0" ] && exec env FLOCKER="$0" flock -en "$0" "$0"
       "$@" || :
           This is useful boilerplate code for shell scripts. Put it at the
           top of the shell script you want to lock and it’ll automatically
           lock itself on the first run. If the environment variable $$FFLLOOCCKKEERR
           is not set to the shell script that is being run, then execute
           fflloocckk and grab an exclusive non-blocking lock (using the script
           itself as the lock file) before re-execing itself with the right
           arguments. It also sets the FFLLOOCCKKEERR environment variable to the
           right value so it doesn’t run again.

       shell> exec 4<>/var/lock/mylockfile; shell> flock -n 4
           This form is convenient for locking a file without spawning a
           subprocess. The shell opens the lock file for reading and writing
           as file descriptor 4, then fflloocckk is used to lock the descriptor.

